A||N||5||artherd||Deralin for 88. Spherical?||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||08-02-2001||03:48 AM||||Well, looking for deralin bushings for the 88 Front (and possibly rear, though Ryane/Held have some TEMPTING spherical metal bearing setups... pricy though.)<P>Any words of wisdom?<P>Thanks!<BR>Ben.||||reg||3
Z||000001||terryk||08-02-2001||12:18 PM||||I have sphericals in the back (Herb Adams). They seem to work nice although I'm not sure the difference is anything cosmic unless you are really wailing on the car alot. They do transmit a bit more road noise to the chassis. They require opeing up the control arm holes and welding. Unless you are going nuts on this car, I wouldn't do it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||shark93726||08-02-2001||11:01 PM||||Polyurethane bushings would work the best and last longer than Delrin bushings.<P>The Fiero Store sells the Prothane brand ones.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Well, looking for deralin bushings for the 88 Front (and possibly rear, though Ryane/Held have some TEMPTING spherical metal bearing setups... pricy though.)<P>Any words of wisdom?<P>Thanks!<BR>Ben.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||08-02-2001||11:27 PM||||polyurathane has much more deflection and resistance than Delrin - how can you say it lasts longer? Delrin is a self lubricating composite that operates just like a bearing with little to no resistance||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||08-02-2001||11:48 PM||||Hi Guys, I should mention that the car is an 88 Formula with a northstar and Getrag transmission.<P>(also looking for some easy 'solid' tranny mounts, though I'll probally just end up fabricating some. Why solid? just to try it :)<P>I already have poly all around, but I dislike the squeaking and creaking (yes, they're lubed, and it's not much, but I'd perfer a silent suspension) <P>Also, even the Prothane flexes too much for my taste. I want NO give :) (but I want a functional bearing too.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||Will||08-03-2001||02:20 AM||||Delrin for the front should be fine. I have  a homemade set in my car. They've lasted 5,000 miles so far.<BR>Delrin is not a composite, and it is my opinion that it still requires lubrication. To that end I have fitted my bushings with zerk fittings.<BR>UHMW or XL UHMW are also very good choices for bushing material. XL is expensive, but has very good NVH characteristics.<P>For the '88 rear I would recommend against Delrin, Poly or UHMW.<BR>The pivots in the '88 rear move in two axes, unlike the control arm pivots of the front suspension.<BR>Because they move in two axes, a single axis pivot like a delrin bushing would end up binding a little bit.<BR>In my opinion, rubber or rod ends are the only proper choices for the '88 rear.<P>||||reg||1
